  "assessment": {
    "rationale": "The envelope gives a clear, local dispatch and signature boundary that can carry a chunk-feed protocol, but it does not itself define CID-mismatch rejection, peer-local accounting records, or retry behavior, so it only partially answers the corrupt-chunk scenario.",
    "strengths": [
      "Explicit positional DAG-CBOR encoding and canonical bytes make envelope interpretation and signing auditable.",
      "Unknown-pCID handling preserves exact bytes and forbids speculative payload parsing, which fits sparse-knowledge forwarding.",
      "The specimen avoids a central pCID registry and keeps feed/CAS semantics in the payload layer instead of hidden global state."
    ],
    "weaknesses": [
      "No native CAS chunk-hash verification or corrupt-chunk proof flow is specified.",
      "Local rejection accounting and retry policy are left to other protocols, which leaves the core scenario under-specified.",
      "The spec is still draft and its long-term freeze/pCID story is not yet settled."
    ],
    "risks": [
      "Peers may replicate bad bytes opaquely until a payload-specific verifier detects the mismatch.",
      "Mandatory signatures do not by themselves guarantee integrity of externally referenced chunk bodies.",
      "Without a shared local-accounting convention, corruption evidence may be surfaced inconsistently across peers."
    ],
    "open_questions": [
      "How should a payload protocol bind chunk CID C and mismatch evidence into signed local records?",
      "Where should peer-local rejection and retry state live: inside payload records, wrapper envelopes, or a separate accounting feed?",
      "What should a receiver do when sig_pcid is unknown even if pcid is known?"
    ],
    "authority_boundary": "Evidence only; does not settle PromiseGrid design."
  }
}
